<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#warring-states-period">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warring States period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 475–<a href="/years/221-bce">221 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Warring States period was the final phase of the Eastern Zhou in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, when seven major states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>, Chu, Qi, Yan, Han, Zhao and Wei, fought for supremacy. It saw mass infantry armies, iron tools, legalist reforms and the work of thinkers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhuangzi">Zhuangzi</a>. It ended when Qin conquered the others and unified China in 221 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warring-states-period.jpg" alt="Warring States period" width="320" height="295" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Philg88 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EN-WarringStatesAll260BCE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The merchant class of the <b>Warring States period</b> was seen as a threat; a law of 214 BCE allowed merchants to be impressed into the army and deported to the frontiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Warring States period</b> its armies were very large; the Zhanguo ce credits it with a million soldiers, a rhetorical figure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its capital, Linzi, became perhaps the largest city of the <b>Warring States period</b>: a speech recorded in the Zhanguo ce credits it with 70,000 households and describes streets so crowded that chariot hubs knocked together, and inhabitants who played music, raced dogs and played the board game liubo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The State of Qin rose from a horse-breeding frontier fief to the most formidable power of the <b>Warring States</b>, and the bureaucratic, legal and military institutions it forged under Shang Yang became, through its unification of China in 221 BCE, the foundations of two thousand years of imperial…</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The regional states created by the Zhou grew into the great powers of the Spring and Autumn and <b>Warring States periods</b>, and the ritual and kinship culture they shared defined membership of the Zhou world, the root of later ideas of a common Chinese civilisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its roots lie in the <b>Warring States period</b>, from the 5th to the 3rd centuries BCE, when the decline of the Zhou order and constant warfare between rival states provoked a flowering of philosophy, the age of the “hundred schools”.</blockquote><a href="/religion/daoism">Daoism · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
